<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="sso"
      toc={[
        {"sso-login", "Setting up SSO login"},
        {"security-notes", "Security notes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="sso">
        <:dek>
          On Team and Enterprise plans, your team signs in to emisar with your own identity
          provider — Google Workspace, Okta, Keycloak, or any compliant OIDC.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This page sets up <strong class="text-zinc-200">SSO login</strong>
        — signing in through your IdP. Provisioning and deprovisioning from the directory is a
        separate piece that builds on a configured connection: <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >directory sync (SCIM)</.link>.
        Both are managed from <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on</strong>
        in your emisar workspace — the Team page lists your connections, and each connection's own
        page holds its configuration.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Where this flow stands">
        <p>
          emisar speaks standards-based OpenID Connect, and the round-trip — OIDC discovery and
          ID-token validation — is verified end to end against a live standards IdP. Vendor-specific
          certification against hosted Okta and JumpCloud tenants is in progress; until it lands,
          treat the per-vendor admin-console steps below as our best read of each provider's UI, not
          a vendor-certified runbook. The emisar side — every step on this page — is
          exactly what you'll see.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso-login">Setting up SSO login</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You need an emisar owner or admin. Open
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on</strong>
        and click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Add provider</strong>; the form opens on its own
        page and walks you through the provider-side app as you pick a provider type.
        <.plan_note tier={:team} />
      </p>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-6">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Create an OAuth / OIDC app at your provider.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              A confidential web application with a client secret. emisar uses standard,
              discovery-based OpenID Connect, so the named providers (Google Workspace, Okta,
              Keycloak) are presets over one flow — there's no separate integration to wait for, and
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Generic OpenID Connect</strong>
              fits anything compliant. When you pick a provider type in the form, the in-app guide
              names the exact admin-console path for it. Leave
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">DPoP</strong>
              (sender-constrained tokens) off — emisar reads the ID token only and never calls an API
              with the access token, so DPoP adds nothing here and would break the token request.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Register emisar's redirect URI on that app.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              The form shows the exact value, with a copy button — it's
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso/callback</code>
              on your emisar host. This is the fixed URI emisar uses; it's never taken from the
              request, so paste it verbatim into your app's allowed redirect / callback URLs.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Fill in the connection and save.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">Back on the emisar form, set:</p>
            <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provider type</strong>
                  and a <strong class="text-zinc-200">Display name</strong>
                  (what your members see — e.g. "Acme Okta").
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Issuer URL</strong>
                  — your provider's OIDC issuer (must be HTTPS). emisar fetches the discovery
                  document from there, so you never paste individual endpoints. The in-app guide
                  shows the shape for the selected provider (your Okta org URL, your Keycloak
                  realm URL, and so on).
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client ID</strong>
                  and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client secret</strong>
                  from the app you just created. The secret is write-only — stored server-side,
                  redacted in the UI, and never rendered back (a sign-in secret has to be replayed to
                  your IdP, so it can't be hashed); at-rest protection is infrastructure-level. When you
                  edit the connection later, leave it blank to keep the current one.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">New-user provisioning</strong>
                  — <span class="text-zinc-300">Auto-provision</span>
                  creates a user on first sign-in; <span class="text-zinc-300">Manual</span>
                  holds each first-time sign-in as a pending request an admin approves. Either way
                  they land at the default role below.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Identifier claim</strong>
                  — leave as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>;
                  Microsoft Entra can use
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
                  instead. emisar accepts only those two, not an arbitrary claim. This is what an
                  identity is bound to (see below), and what SCIM's
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
                  must match for login and directory sync to converge.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Default role for new users</strong>
                  — the role a newly provisioned member lands at (viewer / operator / billing manager / admin). Sync
                  never grants <span class="text-zinc-300">owner</span>; that stays a deliberate
                  human grant. See <.link
                    navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"}
                    class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                  >roles</.link>.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Allowed email domain</strong>
                  (optional) — restricts sign-in to verified addresses on that domain and routes
                  that domain's sign-ins to this connection. Leave blank to accept any address the
                  provider asserts.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  Click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong>
                  before you save. emisar runs a live OIDC discovery round-trip against the issuer
                  and shows the authorization, token, and JWKS endpoints it found — so a wrong issuer
                  or an unreachable provider surfaces here, not at your team's first sign-in.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  Decide whether <strong class="text-zinc-200">this provider satisfies your 2FA
                  requirement</strong>
                  (see below), tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled</strong>, and save. The
                  connection appears in the list, ready to sign in through.
                </span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h3 id="signing-in">How members sign in</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        On the sign-in page, members click <strong class="text-zinc-200">"Continue with single sign-on"</strong>, which sends them to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso</code>; if you set an
        allowed email domain, entering an address on it routes them straight to your provider. They
        authenticate at the IdP and come back signed in.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="subject-not-email">
        Identities are matched by the IdP's subject, never by email
      </.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar binds an SSO login to the provider plus the IdP's stable subject identifier (the
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
        claim, or whichever <strong class="text-zinc-200">identifier claim</strong>
        your provider uses) — <strong class="text-zinc-200">never by email address</strong>.
        Email-matching an SSO login onto an existing account is a known account-takeover vector, so
        we don't do it. The practical consequence: a just-in-time provisioned SSO user is a
        distinct principal, not a merge into a pre-existing account that happens to share
        an email. If an SSO email collides with an existing user, sign-in is refused rather than
        silently linked — an admin links the identity deliberately.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">First login provisions the user</strong>
        at the connection's default role (with the directory as the email authority).
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="sso-mfa">SSO sessions and your MFA requirement</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each connection has a per-provider toggle — <em>"sign-in through this provider satisfies
        the account's 2FA requirement."</em>
        When it's on, an SSO session satisfies an account that
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          requires MFA
        </.link>
        without a second emisar TOTP prompt — your IdP enforces the MFA. Turn it off if you'd
        rather emisar still challenge. Magic-link sign-in is unaffected and is challenged for 2FA
        as usual.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="security-notes">Security notes</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Every SSO sign-in lands in the
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >
              audit log
            </.link>
            with its authentication method, so you can answer "who did this, signed in how".
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
